INT. VILLAGE SCHOOLHOUSE - COMMAND POST - NIGHT

Desks pushed to the walls. A blackboard still bearing a child's arithmetic. A lantern on the teacher's desk. Thunder far off, steady as a drum.

CAPTAIN WALTER BRIGGS (40s, mud to the knee, a map pinned under his palm) studies the table. LIEUTENANT FENN (20s, youthful, shaking pencil) stands behind him. SERGEANT KOWALCZYK (50s, bull neck) leans in the doorway. PRIVATE MBEKI (20s, runner's frame, rifle slung) keeps his eyes on the floor. NEDDY (60s, cook, apron under his greatcoat) ladles stew from a bucket. In the corner, bound to a child's chair, a PRISONER (20s, enemy uniform, bare head) sits with his chin down.

Briggs doesn't look up. He taps the map once.

BRIGGS
Lieutenant. The bridge.

FENN
Still standing, sir.

BRIGGS
Then it's the only thing standing.

He straightens and finally looks at the room. Neddy offers him a tin cup. He takes it. He doesn't drink it.

KOWALCZYK
Captain, the men want to know about the order.

BRIGGS
What order?

KOWALCZYK
The one from Brigade. The one nobody's read aloud.

Mbeki's eyes go to Fenn. Fenn's pencil snaps.

FENN
I read it, Sergeant.

KOWALCZYK
And?

Fenn doesn't answer. He pulls a folded paper from his pocket, smooths it flat on the desk, then slides it toward Briggs. Briggs does not touch it.

BRIGGS
Read it.

FENN
Sir, I...

BRIGGS
Read it, Lieutenant.

Fenn clears his throat. He reads, flat, as if reciting a recipe.

FENN
"Hold the village until relieved. No withdrawal. Prisoners to be processed at discretion."

A long silence. The thunder rolls closer. Neddy sets down his ladle.

NEDDY
Discretion.

KOWALCZYK
It's a word they use when they don't want their names on it.

Briggs finally drinks. He sets the cup down with great care. He crosses to the Prisoner and crouches in front of him. The Prisoner lifts his head. They look at each other for a long moment.

BRIGGS
Does he speak English?

MBEKI
A little, sir.

PRISONER
(thick accent)
I speak. A little.

BRIGGS
How old are you?

PRISONER
Nineteen.

Briggs nods. He stands. His hand finds the holster at his hip, rests on the leather.

FENN
Sir.

BRIGGS
Yes?

FENN
There's the bridge, sir. If we blow it...

BRIGGS
Then we've left the village.

FENN
Yes, sir.

BRIGGS
Against orders.

FENN
(barely)
Yes, sir.

Kowalczyk steps out of the doorway. He crosses to the blackboard and, with his thumb, wipes a child's sum from the slate.

KOWALCZYK
I've been waiting thirty years for an officer to say that out loud.

Briggs lifts his hand off the holster. He unbuckles the Prisoner's wrists.

BRIGGS
Private Mbeki. Take him to the cellar. Give him my blanket.

MBEKI
Yes, sir.

He moves the Prisoner out. At the door, the Prisoner turns, mouths something. Briggs doesn't see.

The first shell lands. The lantern leaps. The blackboard cracks across the middle. Dust sifts from the ceiling.

Neddy covers the stew with his body. Fenn drops under the desk. Kowalczyk draws his pistol and faces the door. Briggs doesn't move. He stands at the table with one hand flat on the map, and he starts to laugh, softly, because it is the only thing left.
